        "heading": "4.1 INFERRING BOTH THE PARAMETERS AND HIDDEN STATES",
        "text": "We can decompose the joint density using the product rule as follows:\np(x,\u03b8|y) = p(x|\u03b8,y)p(\u03b8|y). (7)\nWith the above decomposition we can break down the task of inferring the joint distribution of x,\u03b8 into two sub-tasks of inferring separately the distributions p(\u03b8|y) and p(x|\u03b8,y). Samples of x can then be drawn given samples of \u03b8. Note that the task of inferring p(\u03b8|y) can be carried out, sample efficiently, using any off-the-shelf NLFI method. Thus, the challenge is to develop a NLFI strategy for inferring p(x|\u03b8,y). The advantage of applying NLFI to infer p(x|\u03b8,y) rather than p(x,\u03b8|y) is that for the former case we can exploit the Markov property to escape the curse-of-dimensionality.\nThis is a non-standard approach of drawing from the joint distribution, simply because there did not exist any method, before the advent of NLFI, which can target p(\u03b8|y) without drawing x. Any classical pseudo-marginal sampling method, targeting \u03b8 (Beaumont, 2003; Andrieu & Roberts, 2009), that uses the marginal likelihood p(y|\u03b8), or its unbiased estimate, in the MCMC acceptance step invariably also draws x.\nNote that if the observation density is known analytically, then a standard Bootstrap (Gordon et al., 1995) SMC algorithm can be used, since it does not require a tractable transition density, to infer p(x|\u03b8,y) \u2248 psmc(x|y,\u03b8), given an estimate of p(\u03b8|y) obtained using NLFI. The posterior predictive distribution can then be evaluated as follows:\np(y\u2217|y) = \u222b p(y\u2217|x,\u03b8)p(x,\u03b8|y)dxd\u03b8\n\u2248 \u222b p(y\u2217|x,\u03b8)psmc(x|y,\u03b8)p(\u03b8|y)dxd\u03b8. (8)\nAlthough in certain practical applications the observation density remains tractable, the SMC algorithm however would require a large number of simulations from the model, thus defeating the purpose of NLFI. This is the reason we consider learning an approximation of p(x|\u03b8,y) using a neural density estimator, which we turn to next."
